      "text": "War History Online:\nHerman Davis was born on January 3, 1888, in Manila, Arkansas. A quiet man from a small farming community, Davis didn’t seek attention or fame. But his reputation as a skilled marksman made him stand out even before  World War I. When the U.S. entered the war in 1917, Davis, at almost 30  years old, decided to enlist. Though he could have avoided combat duty because of his age and height (he was a bit under the typical size  requirements), he went straight into service, joining Company I of the 113th Infantry Regiment, 29th Division.\nDavis  quickly proved himself during his training with a Springfield rifle, impressing his superiors with near-perfect accuracy. Sent to the front lines in France, he found himself fighting in some of the war’s most intense battles. One of his most famous feats took place at the  Battle of Belleau Wood in 1918. During this fierce battle, Davis spotted a German machine gun nest firing on his comrades from a range of about 1,000 yards. With open sights and no fancy scopes, Davis calmly aimed his Springfield rifle and took out five enemy soldiers, effectively silencing the gunner. Afterward, when others praised him, he humbly called it “just a good shootin' distance,” brushing it off as if it were  nothing special.\nAnother story that solidified Davis’s reputation came from his role in the  Meuse-Argonne Offensive. This massive offensive, one of the last of the  war, took place in September and October 1918. During one particularly  intense engagement, Davis went ahead of his squad under heavy fire and  took out several German snipers who were hidden and targeting American soldiers. This act saved lives and allowed his unit to move forward.\nDavi  was awarded the Distinguished Service Cross for his actions in the  Meuse-Argonne. Additionally, his bravery caught the attention of the French government, which awarded him the Croix de Guerre and the  Médaille Militaire for his valor in battle. Despite these honors, Davis kept his head down, never boasting about his feats. When he returned to Arkansas after the war, he didn’t seek recognition or parades. Locals  eventually learned about his deeds, and a statue was raised in his honor in his hometown.\nHerman Davis lived quietly after the war, but his health declined, partly due to the toll of wartime exposure to gas. He died of tuberculosis on  January 5, 1923, just two days after his 35th birthday.\n\nHerman Davis, Farm Boy, War Hero:\nhttps://t.co/OHJUU4htSQ\n@IncognitoMike72 @AndInTheEnd2 @randyupton33",
